FAQ = [
 ("Do Wrangler JL Unlimited running boards fit the Gladiator?",
  "Do not assume so. The Gladiator is based on the Wrangler JL, but Wikipedia lists a 137.3 in wheelbase for the JT against 118.4 in for the 4-door Wrangler Unlimited, a gap of 18.9 in. The makers we read sell the two as separate parts. Westin's Outlaw Drop is 20-14165 for the 2020–2026 Gladiator and 20-14065 for the 2018–2026 Wrangler 4-door, and Quadratec lists Rough Country's 90762, Go Rhino's 684451687T and AMP Research's 75135-01A for the Gladiator JT by name. Buy a step whose title or fit list names the Gladiator JT and your model year."),
 ("Does my Gladiator already have factory rock rails?",
  "Look under the doors, because it depends on trim and year. Jeep's 2025 Gladiator press kit lists steel rock rails for the cab and bed corners on the Willys and the Rubicon, and steel rock rails for the cab on the Mojave. The Mojave X and Rubicon X add steel rock rails for the bed corners. In the kit we read, rock rails are named only under those trims. Wikipedia also notes standard rock rails on the Willys and black side steps on the Texas Trail edition. We did not read a Jeep release for every earlier model year, so treat this as a guide and check your own truck or its window sticker."),
 ("Do I have to remove the factory rock rails to fit side steps?",
  "For one pick, yes. For the rest, we could not confirm. Quadratec's page for Rough Country's 90762 Contoured Drop Steps says that if the Jeep has factory Rubicon rock rails, they have to be removed before the steps go on. The Quadratec pages for Go Rhino's OE Xtreme and AMP Research's PowerStep, Westin's Outlaw Drop page and the Fat Bob's Garage pages for Rough Country's power boards and rock sliders say nothing about factory rails either way. Westin lists its Gladiator part as a body mount. If you own a Willys, Rubicon or Mojave, ask the seller in writing whether the kit replaces the rails or bolts beside them."),
 ("Are power running boards worth it on a Gladiator?",
  "They are the only type that gives a low step without a permanent clearance cost. A fixed step hangs under the rocker all the time, and on a truck with a 137.3 in wheelbase the middle of the truck is what meets a ledge first. A power step drops when a door opens and folds up when it closes. The price is real: Quadratec lists AMP Research's PowerStep 75135-01A at $2,099.99, and Fat Bob's Garage lists Rough Country's power boards at $1,099.95. The retailers estimate 3 to 5 hours of installation. If the truck stays on pavement, a fixed drop step does the same job for a fraction of that."),
 ("What is the difference between a tube step, a drop step and a rock slider?",
  "A tube step, or nerf bar, is a tube along the rocker with a pad at each door. Go Rhino's 4 in OE Xtreme and TAC's 3 in side bars are that type. A drop step puts the pad below the bar for a lower first step: Westin's Outlaw Drop has a 4 in drop plate, and Rough Country's Contoured Drop Steps tuck a flush rail against the body with a step under each door. A rock slider is armor first. Rough Country's 90802 is 1/8 in steel, bolts to the frame and has a top you can stand on. Sliders give the highest step and the most protection. Drop steps give the lowest step and the least clearance."),
 ("How much weight can Gladiator side steps hold?",
  "It depends on the product, and only three of the seven picks publish a figure on the pages we read. Fat Bob's Garage lists Rough Country's power boards at up to 660 lb on each side. Quadratec lists AMP Research's PowerStep at 600 lb per side and Rough Country's Contoured Drop Steps as capable of handling up to 465 lb, without saying whether that is per side. The Go Rhino, Westin and rock slider pages give no rating, and TAC's listing title gives none. Ask the seller for a figure if a heavy rider will use the step, and never use a step as a jacking point unless the maker says it is built for that."),
 ("Do I need to drill to install Gladiator side steps?",
  "Not for the picks whose pages address it. Westin lists the Outlaw Drop as a bolt-on installation with no drilling required and with brackets included. Quadratec describes Go Rhino's OE Xtreme as a channel-mount, non-drill installation. Fat Bob's Garage lists Rough Country's power boards as a no-drill install on factory mounting points, and its 90802 rock sliders as a 100 percent bolt-on frame mount. Quadratec's pages for the 90762 drop steps and the AMP PowerStep list brackets and install times but do not use the words no drilling, and TAC's listing title does not say. Power steps add a wiring job on top of the brackets."),
 ("Will a listing that stops at 2023 or 2025 fit a 2026 Gladiator?",
  "Often, but confirm it. The JT has been one generation since the 2020 model year, and retailers list longer ranges than some Amazon titles do. Quadratec lists Go Rhino's 684451687T for 2020–2027 while the Amazon title says 20-23, and Westin lists its Outlaw Drop 20-14165 for 2020–2026. AMP Research's Amazon title names only 2020–2021, and Rough Country's rock slider title stops at 2022. Send the seller your model year and the part number and ask for a yes in writing. Some listings say 2019. Wikipedia gives April 2019 as the start of production and 2020 as the first model year, so that is the same truck."),
 ("Do these steps fit the Rubicon, the Mojave and the EcoDiesel?",
  "None of the listing titles on this page is limited to a trim, and the retailer pages we read list the Gladiator JT without excluding one. Rough Country's pages say 4WD. What changes by trim is what is already bolted on. Jeep's 2025 press kit lists steel rock rails on the Willys, Rubicon and Mojave, and Rough Country's drop steps need factory Rubicon rails removed. Rough Country's power boards are also listed as not fitting trucks with Mopar's Tire Deflector Bracket, parts 68637397AB and 68640856AB. None of the pages names or excludes the 3.0L EcoDiesel offered in select years, so diesel owners should ask the seller before ordering."),
 ("Will side steps hurt my Gladiator's off-road clearance?",
  "Yes, by however far they hang below the rocker. Approach and departure angles are set by the bumpers, so steps do not change them, but breakover and side clearance drop. That matters more on a Gladiator than on a Wrangler, because the wheelbase is 18.9 in longer than the 4-door Wrangler's by Wikipedia's figures. A drop step such as Westin's Outlaw Drop, with its 4 in drop plate, sits lowest. A straight tube or a slider keeps more, and a power step keeps all of it when the doors are closed. For rocks, pick a frame-mounted slider or keep the factory rock rails your trim came with."),
]

